{
  "id": "COMPOUND:25760 - PROTEIN:2333",
  "verbalization": "Sikokianin A is shown to have a target relationship with Mono-ADP-ribosyltransferase C3 through multiple pathways, with the highest scores in Path 1 and Path 2. These pathways involve Sikokianin A interacting with a common intermediate compound, (1S,2S,5R,6R,7S,8S,11S)-7-hydroxy-5-(hydroxymethyl)-11-[(2R)-1-hydroxypropan-2-yl]-6-methyl-9-oxatricyclo[6.2.1.02,6]undecan-10-one, which is a known target of both Mitogen-activated protein kinase kinase kinase 13 and Serine/threonine-protein kinase Nek9. This intermediate is ultimately targeted by Mono-ADP-ribosyltransferase C3, suggesting a shared mechanism of action involving the enzyme's role in ADP-ribosylation. The evidence supports a potential mechanism where Sikokianin A may inhibit Mono-ADP-ribosyltransferase C3 through the activation of downstream signaling pathways, possibly by modulating the activity of the intermediate compound. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Sikokianin A in targeting the enzyme, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
